f 1071 "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}$ at the point $A(-1,-1)$ in the direction towards the point $B(2,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518124 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "36bc1d86-9d95-5fad-8592-9df3687ca62a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518124 #edb/ref 1074 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,0⟩-⟨-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}}}⟨3,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟨3,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2},\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8x(2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}),\\,36{y}^{3}-24{x}^{2}y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(-1,-1) & =⟨8(-1)\\cdot (2(-1)^{2}-3(-1)^{2}),36(-1)^{3}-24(-1)^{2}(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(-1,-1) & =∇f(-1,-1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨8,-12⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}⟩ \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518155 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518155 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86540" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518155 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518155 #edb/ref 1071 "In what direction does the function $g(x,y,z)=4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)$ increase most rapidly at the point $(2,-1,0)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518155 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d63b608-6564-54eb-94c2-8afc07416275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186518155 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{z}^{2}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,-\\frac{2}{x-y},\\,8z⟩ \\\\ ∇g(2,-1,0) & =⟨\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,-\\frac{2}{2-(-1)},\\,8\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,-\\frac{2}{3},\\,0⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523202 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523207 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523202 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523202 #edb/ref 1071 "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(1,1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523202 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48a5b0a5-ecb9-5e4e-bbb7-138d96fc81cd" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523202 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(4\\cdot (1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))+4\\cdot 1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+(2(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =8 \\sin{(1 )}\\text{ }i-2 \\cos{(1 )}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $-8 \\sin{(1 )}\\,i+2 \\cos{(1 )}\\,j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523211 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523228 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523211 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523211 #edb/ref 1071 "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{3}y^{2}z^{4})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1,1)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523211 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d226b9a-26b3-5e84-bd16-a03937995368" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523211 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{3}y^{2}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{3}y^{2}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{3}y^{2}z^{4}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{3}y^{2}z^{4}}\\cdot 3x^{2}y^{2}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{3}y^{2}z^{4}}\\cdot 2x^{3}y^{1}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{3}y^{2}z^{4}}\\cdot 4x^{3}y^{2}z^{3}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{3}{x},\\,\\frac{2}{y},\\,\\frac{4}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,1,1) & =⟨\\frac{3}{1},\\,\\frac{2}{1},\\,\\frac{4}{1}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{∇f(1,1,1)}{‖∇f(1,1,1)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(4)^{2}}}⟨3,\\,2,\\,4⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{3 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{2 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{4 \\sqrt{29}}{29}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the unit direction of $⟨\\frac{3 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{2 \\sqrt{29}}{29},\\,\\frac{4 \\sqrt{29}}{29}⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523221 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523225 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523221 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523221 #edb/ref 1071 "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(1,1,3)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523221 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a6e0bfcc-2db2-5134-b63f-ed16edd03447" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523221 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}}\\cdot 2x^{1}y^{1}z^{6},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}}\\cdot x^{2}y^{0}z^{6},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{2}y^{1}z^{6}}\\cdot 6x^{2}y^{1}z^{5}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{2}{x},\\,\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{6}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(1,1,3) & =⟨\\frac{2}{1},\\,\\frac{1}{1},\\,\\frac{6}{3}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨2,\\,1,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{∇f(1,1,3)}{‖∇f(1,1,3)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}+(2)^{2}}}⟨2,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the unit direction of $⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{3}⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523227 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523222 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523222 #edb/ref 1071 "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4})$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(2,2,2)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523222 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aaff19d2-1089-5ffb-bb3e-92011188d5ba" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523222 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}}\\cdot 6x^{5}y^{4}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}}\\cdot 4x^{6}y^{3}z^{4},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{6}y^{4}z^{4}}\\cdot 4x^{6}y^{4}z^{3}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{6}{x},\\,\\frac{4}{y},\\,\\frac{4}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(2,2,2) & =⟨\\frac{6}{2},\\,\\frac{4}{2},\\,\\frac{4}{2}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨3,\\,2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =-\\frac{∇f(2,2,2)}{‖∇f(2,2,2)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}}⟨3,\\,2,\\,2⟩ \\\\ & =⟨-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the unit direction of $⟨-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17},\\,-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523223 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523195 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523223 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523223 #edb/ref 1071 "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3})$ decrease most rapidly at the point $(3,3,3)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523223 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1292b8d-2b01-5533-896d-f2505b0485b1" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523223 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(3,3,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}}\\cdot 4x^{3}y^{1}z^{3},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}}\\cdot x^{4}y^{0}z^{3},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{4}y^{1}z^{3}}\\cdot 3x^{4}y^{1}z^{2}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{4}{x},\\,\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{3}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(3,3,3) & =⟨\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{3}{3}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =-\\frac{∇f(3,3,3)}{‖∇f(3,3,3)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{(\\frac{4}{3})^{2}+(\\frac{1}{3})^{2}+(1)^{2}}}⟨\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3},\\,1⟩ \\\\ & =⟨-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{26}}{13},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{26}}{26}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the unit direction of $⟨-\\frac{2 \\sqrt{26}}{13},\\,-\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26},\\,-\\frac{3 \\sqrt{26}}{26}⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523226 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523217 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523226 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523226 #edb/ref 1071 "In what unit direction does the function $f(x,y,z)=\\ln (x^{5}y^{2}z^{2})$ increase most rapidly at the point $(3,2,2)$?" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523226 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d24d013a-ecfd-5200-be0e-2480338379cf" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523226 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y,z)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(3,2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\ln (x^{5}y^{2}z^{2})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\ln (x^{5}y^{2}z^{2})),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\ln (x^{5}y^{2}z^{2}))⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{1}{x^{5}y^{2}z^{2}}\\cdot 5x^{4}y^{2}z^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{5}y^{2}z^{2}}\\cdot 2x^{5}y^{1}z^{2},\\,\\frac{1}{x^{5}y^{2}z^{2}}\\cdot 2x^{5}y^{2}z^{1}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{5}{x},\\,\\frac{2}{y},\\,\\frac{2}{z}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(3,2,2) & =⟨\\frac{5}{3},\\,\\frac{2}{2},\\,\\frac{2}{2}⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{5}{3},\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding unit direction is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{∇f(3,2,2)}{‖∇f(3,2,2)‖} \\\\ & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(\\frac{5}{3})^{2}+(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}}⟨\\frac{5}{3},\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\ & =⟨\\frac{5 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the unit direction of $⟨\\frac{5 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43},\\,\\frac{3 \\sqrt{43}}{43}⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523235 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186124459.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186523235 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid "571dc50a-1c8e-5ef3-85a4-84e5e40856c5" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548195 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548192 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548195 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548195 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} y + 2 x e^{y}$ at the point $A(2,0)$ in the direction towards the point $B(4,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548195 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4d2c788-fb3a-5910-a90d-014c2de97596" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186548195 #edb/ref 1074 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨4,1⟩-⟨2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}}}⟨2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x^{2} y + 2 x e^{y}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4 x^{2} y + 2 x e^{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8 x y + 2 e^{y},2 x \\left(2 x + e^{y}\\right)⟩ \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =⟨8 (2) (0) + 2 e^{(0)},4 (2)^{2} + 2 (2) e^{(0)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(2,0) & =∇f(2,0)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨2,20⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5},\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{24 \\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443]